## Primary-index match

The case-insensitive `perf|kernel-tools|linux-tools` search identified this
exact ARMv7 package block in the unified primary index:

name=linux-kernel-perf
arch=armv7l
version=6.12.80-0
vcs=platform/kernel/linux-rpi#b3c2c75beb116df10233fbf0c2c19d50cc03edf7
summary=The perf performance counter tool
location=armv7l/linux-kernel-perf-6.12.80-0.armv7l.rpm
metadata_sha256=cd9891e8197f5e0269a5cd7dd3990bf1bcaea048e06a015dc305f7e9cd05a864
provides=linux-kernel-perf,perf,/usr/bin/perf,/usr/bin/trace

The package version exactly matches board kernel `6.12.80-arm-rpi4-v7l`.

## Dependency inspection

$ readelf -d extracted/usr/bin/perf | grep -E 'NEEDED|RPATH|RUNPATH'
NEEDED libunwind.so.8
NEEDED libunwind-arm.so.8
NEEDED libpthread.so.0
NEEDED librt.so.1
NEEDED libm.so.6
NEEDED libdl.so.2
NEEDED libz.so.1
NEEDED libelf.so.1
NEEDED libdw.so.1
NEEDED libcrypto.so.3
NEEDED libLLVM.so.22.1
NEEDED libstdc++.so.6
NEEDED liblzma.so.5
NEEDED libc.so.6
NEEDED ld-linux.so.3
NEEDED libgcc_s.so.1

Board `rpm -q --whatprovides` resolved all direct libraries except
`libLLVM.so.22.1`; `/usr/bin/perl` was also absent. The primary index resolved
those to `libllvm` and `perl`; closure inspection then found `libgdbm.so.3`
missing and resolved it to `gdbm`. Other required libraries were already
provided by the board image.

