## Functional self-test

$ perf record -F 99 -g -o /tmp/selftest.data -- sleep 2 && perf report -i /tmp/selftest.data --stdio | head
[ perf record: Woken up 1 times to write data ]
[ perf record: Captured and wrote 0.003 MB /tmp/selftest.data ]
Error:
The /tmp/selftest.data data has no samples!

The exact prescribed command exercised recording but the sleeping workload
had no scheduled CPU sample. This was not a perf_event kernel error. A bounded
CPU-active confirmation using the same event/frequency/callgraph settings was
therefore run:

$ perf record -F 99 -g -o /tmp/selftest-cpu2.data -- dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null bs=1048576 count=512
512+0 records in
512+0 records out
536870912 bytes (537 MB) copied, 0.413991 s, 1.3 GB/s
[ perf record: Captured and wrote 0.007 MB /tmp/selftest-cpu2.data (41 samples) ]
record_rc=0
# Samples: 41 of event 'task-clock:ppp'
# Total Lost Samples: 0
stats_SAMPLE_events=41

functional_validation=PASS
kernel_perf_event_support=PASS

